            <ArticleTitle>Genitourinary imaging in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1381-424</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Genitourinary imaging in children has gone through a metamorphosis in the past 30 years, paralleling technical advances in sonography, CT scanning, and MR imaging. The imaging sequences for hydronephrosis and UTI are still controversial and require outcomes analysis to determine which studies are most beneficial and efficacious.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
